<check_type:"Unix">

<if>
  <condition type:"AND">
    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 is installed"
      file        : "/etc/system-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*Red Hat Enterprise Linux.*release"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*Red Hat Enterprise Linux.*release @PLATFORM_VERSION@"
    </custom_item>
  </condition>

  <then>
    <report type:"PASSED">
      description : "CIS_Red_Hat_Enterprise_Linux_10_STIG_v1.0.0_CAT_II.audit from CIS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 STIG v1.0.0"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      show_output : YES
    </report>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "release key 2"
          cmd         : "/bin/rpm -q --queryformat \"%{SUMMARY}\\n\" gpg-pubkey"
          expect      : "[\\s]*Red[\\s]+Hat,[\\s]+Inc\\.[\\s]+\\(release[\\s]+key[\\s]+2\\)[\\s]+<security@redhat\\.com>[\\s]+public[\\s]+key[\\s]*"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "auxiliary key"
          cmd         : "/bin/rpm -q --queryformat \"%{SUMMARY}\\n\" gpg-pubkey"
          expect      : "[\\s]*Red[\\s]+Hat,[\\s]+Inc\\.[\\s]+\\(auxiliary[\\s]+key[\\s]+3\\)[\\s]+<security@redhat\\.com>[\\s]+public[\\s]+key[\\s]*"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "release key 4"
          cmd         : "/bin/rpm -q --queryformat \"%{SUMMARY}\\n\" gpg-pubkey"
          expect      : "[\\s]*Red[\\s]+Hat,[\\s]+Inc\\.[\\s]+\\(release[\\s]+key[\\s]+4\\)[\\s]+<security@redhat\\.com>[\\s]+public[\\s]+key[\\s]*"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "key fingerprint 2"
          cmd         : "/bin/sq inspect /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release"
          expect      : "^(?i)[\\s]*Fingerprint[\\s]*:[\\s]*567E347AD0044ADE55BA8A5F199E2F91FD431D51[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "key fingerprint auxiliary"
          cmd         : "/bin/sq inspect /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release"
          expect      : "^(?i)[\\s]*Fingerprint[\\s]*:[\\s]*7E4624258C406535D56D6F135054E4A45A6340B3[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "key fingerprint 4"
          cmd         : "/bin/sq inspect /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release"
          expect      : "^(?i)[\\s]*Fingerprint[\\s]*:[\\s]*FCD355B305707A62DA143AB6E422397E50FE8467A2A95343D246D6276AFEDF8F[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.10 RHEL-10-001020"
          info        : "RHEL 10 must ensure cryptographic verification of vendor software packages.

GROUP ID: V-280931RULE ID: SV-280931r1197213

Cryptographic verification of vendor software packages ensures that all software packages are obtained from a valid source and protects against spoofing that could lead to installation of malware on the system. Red Hat cryptographically signs all software packages, including updates, with a GNU Privacy Guard (GPG) key to verify that they are valid."
          solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to ensure cryptographic verification of vendor software packages.

Install Red Hat package-signing keys on the system and verify their fingerprints match vendor values.

Insert the RHEL 10 installation disc or attach the RHEL 10 installation image to the system. Mount the disc or image to make the contents accessible inside the system.

Assuming the mounted location is \"/media/cdrom\", use the following command to copy the Red Hat GPG key file onto the system:

$ sudo cp /media/cdrom/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/

Import Red Hat GPG keys from the key file into the system keyring:

$ sudo rpm --import /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release

Using the steps listed in the Check Text, confirm the newly imported keys show as installed on the system and verify their fingerprints match vendor values."
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.5,800-171r3|03.04.05,800-53|CM-5(3),800-53r5|CM-14,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-003992,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.19,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.32,ITSG-33|CM-5(3),LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.2.3,NESA|T5.1.1,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.1,NESA|T7.5.3,NESA|T7.6.3,QCSC-v1|7.2,Rule-ID|SV-280931r1197213_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-001020,Vuln-ID|V-280931"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CHECK
      description : "1.100 RHEL-10-400065"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must be configured so that the \"/etc/shadow\" file is group-owned by \"root\".

GROUP ID: V-281030
RULE ID: SV-281030r1165445

The \"/etc/shadow\" file stores password hashes. Protection of this file is critical for system security."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 so that the group of the \"/etc/shadow\" file is set to \"root\" by running the following command:

$ sudo chgrp root /etc/shadow"
      reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171r3|03.01.02,800-53|AC-3,800-53r5|AC-3,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-000213,CN-L3|8.1.4.2(f),CN-L3|8.1.4.11(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.2(c),CN-L3|8.5.3.1,CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.15,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.5,ITSG-33|AC-3,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T4.2.1,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T5.4.5,NESA|T5.5.4,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.2,NESA|T7.5.3,NIAv2|AM3,NIAv2|SS29,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-281030r1165445_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-400065,TBA-FIISB|31.1,Vuln-ID|V-281030"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/shadow"
      group       : "root"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CHECK
      description : "1.101 RHEL-10-400070"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must be configured so that the \"/etc/shadow-\" file is owned by \"root\".

GROUP ID: V-281031
RULE ID: SV-281031r1165448

The \"/etc/shadow-\" file is a backup file of \"/etc/shadow\", and as such contains the list of local system accounts and password hashes. Protection of this file is critical for system security."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 so that the owner of the \"/etc/shadow-\" file is set to \"root\" by running the following command:

$ sudo chown root /etc/shadow-"
      reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171r3|03.01.02,800-53|AC-3,800-53r5|AC-3,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-000213,CN-L3|8.1.4.2(f),CN-L3|8.1.4.11(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.2(c),CN-L3|8.5.3.1,CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.15,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.5,ITSG-33|AC-3,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T4.2.1,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T5.4.5,NESA|T5.5.4,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.2,NESA|T7.5.3,NIAv2|AM3,NIAv2|SS29,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-281031r1165448_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-400070,TBA-FIISB|31.1,Vuln-ID|V-281031"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/shadow-"
      owner       : "root"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CHECK
      description : "1.102 RHEL-10-400075"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must be configured so that the \"/etc/shadow-\" file is group-owned by \"root\".

GROUP ID: V-281032
RULE ID: SV-281032r1165451

The \"/etc/shadow-\" file is a backup file of \"/etc/shadow\", and as such contains the list of local system accounts and password hashes. Protection of this file is critical for system security."
