    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.59 RHEL-10-200644"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must authenticate the remote logging server for off-loading audit logs via \"rsyslog\".

GROUP ID: V-280987RULE ID: SV-280987r1195371

Information stored in one location is vulnerable to accidental or incidental deletion or alteration.

Off-loading is a common process in information systems with limited audit storage capacity.

RHEL 10 installation media provides \"rsyslogd\", a system utility providing support for message logging. Support for both internet and Unix domain sockets enables this utility to support both local and remote logging. Coupling this utility with \"gnutls\" (a secure communications library implementing the Secure Sockets Layer (SSL), Transport Layer Security (TLS), and Datagram TLS (DTLS) protocols) creates a method to securely encrypt and off-load auditing.

The \"rsyslog\" supported authentication modes include:

 - anon - Anonymous authentication.
 - x509/fingerprint - Certificate fingerprint authentication.
 - x509/certvalid - Certificate validation only.
 - x509/name - Certificate validation and subject name authentication.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000342-GPOS-00133, SRG-OS-000479-GPOS-00224

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to authenticate the remote logging server for off-loading audit logs by setting the following options in \"/etc/rsyslog.d/99-forwarding.conf\":

streamdriver.authmode=\"x509/name\"

Specify the logserver to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks in the following format:streamdriver.permittedpeer=\"rsyslog.server.example.com\""
      reference   : "800-53|AU-4(1),800-53r5|AU-4(1),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001851,CSF|PR.DS-4,CSF|PR.PT-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(b),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.6,ITSG-33|AU-4,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.3.1,NESA|T3.6.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-280987r1195371_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-200644,Vuln-ID|V-280987"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/rsyslog.conf /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf"
      regex       : "(?i)^[^#\\n\\r]*(streamdriver.authmode|streamdriver.permittedpeer|tls.authmode|tls.permittedpeer)"
      expect      : "^Manual Review Required$"
      severity    : MEDIUM
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.6 RHEL-10-000550"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must use a separate file system for \"/var\".

GROUP ID: V-280939RULE ID: SV-280939r1184729

Ensuring that \"/var\" is mounted on its own partition enables the setting of more restrictive mount options. This helps protect system services such as daemons or other programs that use it. It is not uncommon for the \"/var\" directory to contain world-writable directories installed by other software packages."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to use a separate file system for the \"/var\" directory by migrating the \"/var\" path onto a separate file system."
      reference   : "800-53|SC-5,800-53r5|SC-5a.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002385,CSF|DE.CM-1,CSF|PR.DS-4,CSF2.0|DE.CM-01,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-5,ITSG-33|SC-5a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T3.3.1,NIAv2|GS8e,NIAv2|GS10c,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-280939r1184729_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-000550,Vuln-ID|V-280939"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/fstab"
      regex       : "^[^#\\n\\r]+[\\s]+/var[\\s]+"
      expect      : "^[^#\\n\\r]+[\\s]+/var[\\s]+"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.60 RHEL-10-200645"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must encrypt the transfer of audit records off-loaded onto a different system or media from the system being audited via rsyslog.

GROUP ID: V-280988RULE ID: SV-280988r1195374

Information stored in one location is vulnerable to accidental or incidental deletion or alteration.

Off-loading is a common process in information systems with limited audit storage capacity.

RHEL 10 installation media provides \"rsyslogd\", a system utility providing support for message logging. Support for both internet and Unix domain sockets enables this utility to support both local and remote logging. Coupling this utility with \"gnutls\" (a secure communications library implementing the Secure Sockets Layer [SSL], Transport Layer Security [TLS], and Datagram TLS [DTLS] protocols) creates a method to securely encrypt and off-load auditing. When this utility is coupled with the omfwd module, it can use the ossl network stream driver, which leverages the OpenSSL library for Transport Layer Security (TLS) to securely encrypt and off-load auditing.

\"Rsyslog\" supported authentication modes include:

 - anon - Anonymous authentication.
 - x509/fingerprint - Certificate fingerprint authentication.
 - x509/certvalid - Certificate validation only.
 - x509/name - Certificate validation and subject name authentication.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000342-GPOS-00133, SRG-OS-000479-GPOS-00224

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to encrypt off-loaded audit records via rsyslog by enabling TLS within the \"omfwd\" action rule.

Edit the configuration file containing the \"omfwd\" rule (e.g., /etc/rsyslog.d/99-forwarding.conf) and add the tls=\"on\" parameter.

Example:action(type=\"omfwd\"target=\"logserver.example.com\"protocol=\"tcp\"port=\"6514\"tls=\"on\")

After applying the configuration, restart the rsyslog service:

$ sudo systemctl restart rsyslog"
      reference   : "800-53|AU-4(1),800-53r5|AU-4(1),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001851,CSF|PR.DS-4,CSF|PR.PT-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(b),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.6,ITSG-33|AU-4,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.3.1,NESA|T3.6.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-280988r1195374_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-200645,Vuln-ID|V-280988"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/rsyslog.conf /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf"
      regex       : "(?i)^[^#\\n\\r]*(tls=\"on\"|StreamDriver.Mode\\s*=\\s*\"1\")"
      expect      : "^Manual Review Required$"
      severity    : MEDIUM
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.61 RHEL-10-200646"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must encrypt, via the gtls driver, the transfer of audit records off-loaded onto a different system or media from the system being audited via rsyslog.

GROUP ID: V-280989RULE ID: SV-280989r1197222

Information stored in one location is vulnerable to accidental or incidental deletion or alteration.

Off-loading is a common process in information systems with limited audit storage capacity.

RHEL 10 installation media provides \"rsyslogd\", a system utility providing support for message logging. Support for both internet and Unix domain sockets enables this utility to support both local and remote logging. Coupling this utility with \"gnutls\" (a secure communications library implementing the Secure Sockets Layer [SSL], Transport Layer Security [TLS], and Datagram TLS [DTLS] protocols) creates a method to securely encrypt and off-load auditing. When this utility is coupled with the omfwd module, it can use the ossl network stream driver, which leverages the OpenSSL library for Transport Layer Security (TLS) to securely encrypt and off-load auditing.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000342-GPOS-00133, SRG-OS-000479-GPOS-00224

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to explicitly define a TLS driver for rsyslog to use for encrypting off-loaded audit records. The \"ossl\" driver is required for environments operating in FIPS mode.

Add the \"streamdriver\" parameter to the \"omfwd\" action rule in its configuration file (e.g., /etc/rsyslog.d/99-forwarding.conf).

Example:action(type=\"omfwd\"streamdriver=\"ossl\"target=\"logserver.example.com\"protocol=\"tcp\"port=\"6514\"tls=\"on\")

After applying the configuration, restart the rsyslog service:

$ sudo systemctl restart rsyslog"
      reference   : "800-53|AU-4(1),800-53r5|AU-4(1),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001851,CSF|PR.DS-4,CSF|PR.PT-1,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(b),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.6,ITSG-33|AU-4,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.3.1,NESA|T3.6.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-280989r1197222_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-200646,Vuln-ID|V-280989"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/rsyslog.conf /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf"
      regex       : "(?i)^[^#\\n\\r]*StreamDriver\\s*=\\s*\"(gtls|ossl)\""
      expect      : "^Manual Review Required$"
      severity    : MEDIUM
    </custom_item>

